Introduction 
A machine learning model to predict heart disease from clinical data. 
As a biomedical engineering student, I wanted a project that let me apply ML while
using my understanding of physiology to interpret the results, not just optimize numbers.

Dataset
UCI Heart Disease dataset — Cleveland subset. It has 303 patients with 13 clinical features 
like age, chest pain type, cholesterol, and exercise test results. After removing 6 rows with
missing data, I worked with 297 patients.

Data Cleaning
Two columns — ca and thal — had a few missing values, so I dropped those rows since it 
was a small number. I also converted the target variable, which originally had 5 severity
levels, into a binary outcome: disease present or not. That gave me a fairly balanced split 
160 patients without disease, 137 with disease.

EDA / Correlation Analysis
Before modeling, I looked at which features correlated most with disease presence. 
The strongest were thal, ca, oldpeak, exang, and cp — these are things like blocked vessels, 
ST depression during exercise, and chest pain type. Interestingly, cholesterol and blood sugar
the things people usually associate with heart disease barely correlated at all in this
dataset.

Modeling
I trained two models: Logistic Regression as an interpretable baseline, and Random Forest for
comparison. I split the data 80/20 for training and testing, and scaled the features for 
logistic regression since it's sensitive to feature magnitude.

Evaluation
I evaluated using sensitivity, specificity, and ROC-AUC — not just accuracy. In healthcare, missing a real disease case is worse than a false alarm, so recall for the disease class matters most."
Then give the numbers:

-Logistic regression got 83% accuracy, 79% sensitivity, and a ROC-AUC of 0.95
-Random forest got 85% accuracy, also 79% sensitivity, and a ROC-AUC of 0.94
-Both models performed similarly — neither is a clear winner. Logistic regression is
more interpretable, random forest slightly better on accuracy and specificity.

Interpretation
I used Random Forest feature importance and SHAP values to understand why 
the model made its predictions. Chest pain type, thalassemia result, and max heart rate
were the top predictors. SHAP confirmed the direction too — for example, lower max heart rate
during exercise pushed predictions toward disease, which matches real physiology, since
patients with heart disease often can't reach as high a heart rate under exertion.


Limitations
The dataset is small — under 300 patients — and from a single hospital, so it may not 
generalize well. It's also skewed toward male patients. I didn't have an independent dataset 
to validate on either.

Next Steps
I'm now working on deploying this as an interactive app so it's actually usable not just a notebook where someone could enter patient values and get a risk prediction.
This is because early risk detection can support clinical decision-making, and this shows how domain-specific reasoning understanding what these features actually mean physiologically makes ML more trustworthy and interpretable in healthcare, rather than just treating it as a black box.